## SECTION 2: MODULE-BY-MODULE TECHNICAL BREAKDOWN

---

### MODULE 1: VIDEO INGESTION & STREAM MANAGEMENT

#### What It Does
Connects to one or more IP CCTV cameras via RTSP (Real Time Streaming Protocol)
and creates a continuous frame pipeline to feed into all downstream models.

#### Tech Stack
- **OpenCV (`cv2.VideoCapture`)** — primary RTSP reader
- **FFmpeg** — fallback for resilient stream handling
- **asyncio / threading** — parallel stream handling for multi-camera

#### Code Pattern
```python
import cv2
import threading
from queue import Queue

class CameraStream:
    def __init__(self, rtsp_url: str, camera_id: str):
        self.url = rtsp_url
        self.cid = camera_id
        self.q = Queue(maxsize=5)  # bounded — don't accumulate stale frames
        self.cap = cv2.VideoCapture(rtsp_url)
        t = threading.Thread(target=self._reader, daemon=True)
        t.start()

    def _reader(self):
        while True:
            ret, frame = self.cap.read()
            if not ret:
                self.cap.open(self.url)  # auto-reconnect
                continue
            if not self.q.full():
                self.q.put(frame)

    def read(self):
        return self.q.get()
```

#### Problems You WILL Face

**P1.1 — RTSP Stream Drops**
Border areas have unstable networks. RTSP streams will disconnect randomly.
- **Solution:** Auto-reconnect loop (as shown above). Use `cap.open(url)` on failure.
  Add exponential backoff: wait 2s, 4s, 8s before retrying to avoid hammering.

**P1.2 — Frame Lag / Buffer Overflow**
OpenCV's default buffer is 10+ frames. By the time you process, you're analyzing
frames from 5 seconds ago. For border security this is unacceptable.
- **Solution:** Use bounded Queue(maxsize=5) and drop old frames. Skip, don't queue.
  Set `cap.set(cv2.CAP_PROP_BUFFERSIZE, 1)` to minimize OpenCV's internal buffer.

**P1.3 — Multiple Camera CPU Bottleneck**
10 cameras × 25fps = 250 frames/second to process. A single thread dies.
- **Solution:** 1 thread per camera for READING. 1 shared thread pool for INFERENCE.
  Don't run inference on every frame — process every 3rd frame (saves 66% compute
  with <5% accuracy loss for slow-moving targets like border intrusions).

**P1.4 — Variable Camera Resolutions and Codecs**
Different CCTV models output different resolutions (720p, 1080p, 4K) and codecs
(H.264, H.265, MJPEG). Models expect fixed input sizes.
- **Solution:** Always resize to 640×640 before inference.
  Use `cv2.resize(frame, (640, 640))` in the preprocessing step.
  OpenCV handles most codecs automatically.

---

### MODULE 2: HUMAN DETECTION & TRACKING

#### What It Does
Detects every person in the frame, draws bounding boxes, assigns persistent IDs,
and tracks their trajectory across the frame over time.

#### Tech Stack (Recommended)
- **YOLOv8n** (nano) for detection — fastest, 80.4% mAP on COCO persons
- **ByteTrack** for tracking — outperforms DeepSORT in most metrics, no Re-ID model needed
- Alternative: **BoT-SORT** if camera has zoom/pan (handles camera motion compensation)

#### Research Benchmark Data
```
Model         | Speed (ms) | mAP@50 | mAP@50-95
YOLOv8n       |  1.47ms    | 52.9%  | 37.3%     ← deploy this
YOLOv8s       |  2.66ms    | 61.8%  | 44.9%
YOLOv8m       |  5.86ms    | 67.2%  | 50.2%
YOLOv8x       |  13.67ms   | 73.2%  | 53.9%     ← too slow for multi-camera RT

Tracker       | HOTA↑ | MOTA↑ | IDF1↑ | ID Sw.↓
DeepSORT      | 55.2  | 73.5  | 69.1  | 1774
ByteTrack     | 63.1  | 80.3  | 77.3  |  422    ← use this
BoT-SORT      | 64.9  | 80.5  | 79.5  |  305    ← if budget allows
StrongSORT    | 63.5  | 79.6  | 79.7  |  282
```

#### Problems You WILL Face

**P2.1 — False Positives on Animals**
Border areas have deer, dogs, cattle. YOLOv8 trained on COCO detects "person"
and "animal" as separate classes — this should work fine. However low-resolution
distant shots can confuse the model (small animal at distance → "person" label).
- **Solution:** Add minimum bounding box area filter. A human at 50m+ distance
  will have box area <200 pixels. Set `if box_area < 150: skip`.
  Additionally use class confidence threshold >0.45 (not default 0.25).

**P2.2 — Occlusion & ID Switching**
Two people cross paths → tracker loses one ID, re-assigns wrong ID.
ByteTrack handles this better than DeepSORT but still fails on long occlusions.
- **Solution:** For border surveillance static cameras, ByteTrack's motion-based
  association handles 90% of cases. For long occlusions (>5 seconds), accept the
  ID switch and log a "new person detected" event — still valid security alert.

**P2.3 — Crowded Scenes at Checkpoints**
20+ people queuing at a checkpoint → detection degrades (overlap, occlusion).
- **Solution:** YOLOv8 does handle crowds reasonably but accuracy drops to ~65%
  mAP in dense scenes. Use a secondary smaller crop of the crowd region at higher
  resolution for ROI-specific inference.

**P2.4 — Night Detection Degradation**
Standard CCTV nighttime footage → detection accuracy drops 30-40%.
- **Solution:** Apply CLAHE preprocessing BEFORE detection (see Module 7).
  YOLOv8 pretrained on ExDark dataset has improved low-light performance.
  Consider using YOLOv8 DP (dark-pretrained) weights.

---

### MODULE 3: VEHICLE DETECTION & CLASSIFICATION

#### What It Does
Detects vehicles, classifies them (car, truck, motorcycle, bus, bicycle), tracks
their trajectory. Feeds into ANPR module and suspicious vehicle flagging.

#### Tech Stack
- **YOLOv8s** (small) — COCO dataset already includes 8 vehicle classes
- YOLOv8 classes relevant: `car(2), motorcycle(3), airplane(4), bus(5), train(6), truck(7), boat(8)`

#### COCO Vehicle Classes Available Out-of-Box
```
Class ID | Label       | Border Relevance
---------|-------------|------------------
2        | car         | ✅ Primary
3        | motorcycle  | ✅ Infiltration risk
5        | bus         | ✅ Checkpoint
7        | truck       | ✅ Checkpoint
```

#### Problems You WILL Face

**P3.1 — Military Vehicle Misclassification**
BSF vehicles (armored, jeeps, Gypsys) may not match COCO's "car" profile well.
- **Solution:** Fine-tune YOLOv8 on a small dataset of military border vehicles.
  Even 100-200 annotated images via Roboflow will significantly improve accuracy.
  For hackathon: use generic COCO detection and add military classes post-event.

**P3.2 — Partially Visible Vehicles**
A truck entering through a gate — front half visible, rear out of frame.
- **Solution:** YOLOv8 handles partial detections by design. Accept boxes with
  >0.35 confidence even if partially cut off.

**P3.3 — Speed Estimation**
PS doesn't explicitly require it, but vehicle speed adds intelligence.
- **Solution (nice-to-have):** Track bounding box centroid distance between frames.
  Scale by camera FOV angle and estimated distance. As a future enhancement step.

---

### MODULE 4: AUTOMATIC NUMBER PLATE RECOGNITION (ANPR)

#### What It Does
Detects license plates on vehicles and reads the alphanumeric text, logging
every vehicle that passes with timestamp.

#### The Two-Stage Pipeline
```
Stage 1: Plate Detection
  Input: Vehicle crop from Module 3
  Model: YOLOv8 fine-tuned on license plate dataset
  Output: Coordinates of plate bounding box

Stage 2: Character Recognition
  Input: Cropped plate image
  Processing: CLAHE → denoise → perspective correction
  Model: EasyOCR (Indian script support) or PaddleOCR
  Output: Plate string "MH12AB1234"
```

#### Datasets Available
- **Kaggle Indian License Plate Dataset** — 3,000+ Indian plates
- **CCPD (Chinese)** — different but closest to Indian format in diversity
- **OpenANPR** — open source ANPR framework
- **Roboflow Universe** — "Indian Number Plate" dataset, pre-annotated

#### Problems You WILL Face

**P4.1 — Indian Plate Diversity (THE Biggest ANPR Challenge)**
India has no true national standard. Problems include:
```
Issue                    | Example              | Impact
-------------------------|----------------------|--------
Mixed fonts              | IND bold vs normal   | OCR confusion
Bi-lingual scripts       | HR + हरियाणा       | OCR fails Hindi
Fancy plates             | Stylized fonts       | OCR fails
Damaged/dirty plates     | Mud on plate         | Detection fails
Non-standard spacing     | MH·12·AB·1234        | Character split
Temporary plates         | Handwritten          | OCR completely fails
High-security plates     | Chromium hologram    | Reflection artifacts
```
- **Solution:**
  1. Fine-tune EasyOCR on Indian plate dataset. Takes ~2-3 hours on Colab.
  2. Use post-processing regex `[A-Z]{2}[\d]{1,2}[A-Z]{1,3}[\d]{4}` to
     validate extracted text and auto-correct common OCR errors (0→O, 1→I).
  3. Accept partial reads (even 70% confidence partial plate) and flag for
     human review rather than discarding.

**P4.2 — Motion Blur at Speed**
Vehicles crossing at 40+ km/h → plate blurred.
- **Solution:** Use frame sampling — trigger ANPR only when vehicle speed
  (measured by bounding box delta) is below threshold. At 5-10 km/h (checkpoint
  speed) blur is negligible. For high-speed: use deblurring filter (Wiener filter).

**P4.3 — Camera Angle**
CCTV cameras mounted high at border gates create a top-down perspective.
Plate text appears skewed → OCR accuracy drops ~25%.
- **Solution:** Apply perspective correction: detect plate corners, apply
  `cv2.getPerspectiveTransform()` to de-skew before OCR.

**P4.4 — Night ANPR**
IR illumination on CCTV makes plates appear differently under night mode.
- **Solution:** Train separate OCR model on IR-illuminated plate images.
  Many CCTV cameras have dedicated IR-ANPR mode — check if cameras support it.

**P4.5 — Watchlist Matching**
How to flag a vehicle with a stolen/blacklisted plate?
- **Solution:** Maintain SQLite/PostgreSQL table of flagged plates.
  Levenshtein distance matching (fuzzy match, distance ≤ 2) to catch partial
  reads or slight typos. Flag if Levenshtein(detected, flagged) < 2.

---

### MODULE 5: FACIAL RECOGNITION SYSTEM (FRS)

#### What It Does
Detects faces in the frame, extracts facial embeddings (512-dim vectors), and
matches against a registered watchlist database. Fires alert on known-person match.

#### Tech Stack
- **Detection:** RetinaFace (best accuracy for small/angled faces)
  or MTCNN (faster but slightly less accurate)
- **Embedding:** InsightFace ArcFace model (state-of-art: 99.83% LFW accuracy)
- **Similarity:** Cosine similarity with threshold 0.60-0.65
- **Database:** FAISS for million-scale fast vector search

#### FRS Pipeline
```python
from insightface.app import FaceAnalysis
import numpy as np, faiss

# Setup (once at startup)
app = FaceAnalysis(name='buffalo_l')  # ArcFace backbone
app.prepare(ctx_id=0)  # GPU

# Enrollment (add person to watchlist)
def enroll_face(image, person_id, name):
    faces = app.get(image)
    embedding = faces[0].normed_embedding  # 512-dim L2-normalized vector
    index.add(embedding.reshape(1, -1))    # FAISS index
    metadata.append({'id': person_id, 'name': name})

# Recognition (real-time)
def recognize(frame):
    faces = app.get(frame)
    for face in faces:
        emb = face.normed_embedding.reshape(1, -1)
        D, I = index.search(emb, k=1)  # nearest neighbor
        if D[0][0] > 0.65:             # cosine similarity threshold
            return metadata[I[0][0]]    # matched person
        else:
            return "UNKNOWN"
```

#### Research Accuracy Benchmarks
```
Condition                  | Accuracy | Notes
---------------------------|----------|------------------------------------------
Controlled indoor lighting | 99.8%    | Near-perfect (airport kiosk conditions)
Outdoor daylight           | 95-97%   | Good
Outdoor night/low-light    | 78-85%   | Degrades significantly
Faces with masks           | 60-72%   | Half-face only
Faces with sunglasses      | 45-65%   | Glasses occlude key periorbital features
Faces at >45° angle        | 72-82%   | Profile views are hard
Faces at >60m distance     | 55-70%   | Low resolution = poor embedding quality
```

#### Problems You WILL Face

**P5.1 — Small Faces at Distance (THE Critical Issue)**
Border cameras are often 10-50m from subjects. Face subtends only 20-40 pixels.
InsightFace requires minimum ~80×80 pixel face box for reliable embedding.
- **Solution:**
  1. Super-resolution preprocessing on face crops (Real-ESRGAN or GFPGAN).
     Apply before embedding extraction for distant small faces.
  2. Set minimum face size filter: only attempt FRS if face box > 50×50 px.
     Ignore smaller faces (flag as "unidentifiable" in log).
  3. Use camera optical zoom channel if available on border PTZ cameras.

**P5.2 — Outdoor Lighting Variation**
Sunrise/sunset harsh sidelong lighting → half face in shadow.
Midday harsh sunrays → blown highlights on forehead.
- **Solution:** Histogram equalization + CLAHE on face crop before embedding.
  Training with augmented datasets that include lighting variation helps generalization.

**P5.3 — Demographic Bias in Pretrained Models**
ArcFace (pretrained on MS1MV2) has demonstrated higher error rates on South Asian
and darker-skinned individuals in some studies (up to 3-5% more false rejections).
For a BSF deployment this is a critical operational concern.
- **Solution:**
  1. Fine-tune on South Asian face dataset (VGGFace2 has some representation).
  2. Lower confidence threshold for demographic groups at higher risk of false rejection.
  3. Always maintain "human-in-the-loop" — FRS suggests, human confirms.
  4. Log all false positives for continuous model improvement post-deployment.

**P5.4 — Spoofing (Printed Photo / Digital Screen)**
A threat actor could hold up a printed photo of an authorized person.
- **Solution:** Liveness detection module:
  - **Passive liveness:** Detect texture differences between real skin vs. printed paper
    using FAS (Face Anti-Spoofing) models (e.g., MiniFASNet).
  - **Active liveness:** Prompt user to blink/turn head (for checkpoint use only).
  Add MiniFASNet before any recognition attempt.

**P5.5 — Watchlist Database Size**
BSF might have a watchlist of 100,000+ persons of interest.
- **Solution:** FAISS flat index handles 1M embeddings with <10ms search time
  on CPU. For 100K entries, FAISS is near-instant. No scaling concern here.

**P5.6 — Privacy & Legal**
This is a government border security application. No civilian privacy law
prevents FRS use at military border installations in India.
For the hackathon, frame as: "FRS for registered POI matching only,
not mass surveillance. All enrollments require human authorization."

